h system is performing  badly the clinic owner has spent decades  crusading for a parallel private system  to ease stress on our public hospitals  and save lives he says the promise  was that we would have a universal  system where everyone was treated but  people are dying on weightless just last  month the bc court justice agreed the  two-tier ban in that province imposes  suffering on patients stuck in the  public cue but as it stands medical  services offered under the canada health  act cannot also be covered by private  insurance and the mere mention of  privatization creates conflict and  political polarization we just can't as  a province keep doing the same thing  and expecting a different result the  national health care crisis has some  leaders in ontario alberta saskatchewan  and quebec exploring relationships with  private companies but it's unclear in  exactly what capacity and at who's cost  in germany citizens who have the money  can opt out of the country system for  substitute of coverage a popular choice  in australia is to pay for insurance to  access private facilities and even the  uk which has a revered universal health  care system offers private options still  these countries aren't immune to post  pandemic backlogs or wait times  privatization of anything would make no  difference in the emergency room public  health physician dr michael ratchlis  wants to see canada focus on optimizing  existing facilities like this one of the  largest surgical centers in north  america that sits just outside downtown  toronto we've got the solutions of the  public sector but when people don't hear  about them if you don't know what's on  the menu you can't ask for it managing  patient levels should start outside the  er explains this harvard health expert  the nature of those peaks and valleys  are elective scheduled admissions so if  you smooth those peaks and valleys you  can dramatically improve your operation  with the immediate goal of alleviating  burnout increasing retention and  improving patient care jamie morocco  global news toronto  a new report by the alzheimer's society  of canada found over one million  canadians could be diagnosed with  dementia by 2030. that almost doubles  the number of cases in canada right now  and the number could triple by 2050. in  many cases we know family members are  caring for their loved ones the study  says in the next 30 years those roles  will equal about 700 000 full-time jobs  the best way  to advance is to prevent as many cases  as possible that requires reducing risk  factors such as substance abuse  traumatic injuries and pollution the  study authors admit this requires  individual as well as community and  government support to succeed  rain and cooler temperatures bringing  some relief to crews fighting the  wildfire burning in jasper national park  the fire now mapped at an area of about  6 000 hectares is down from about 8 000  hectares yesterday it's burning about 16  kilometers north of the resort town of  jasper at this point there are no  evacuation orders or alerts in place  but visitors are being asked to  reconsider their plans the flames have  damaged power lines into town there's no  word on when electricity will be fully  restored  a community's grief next we speak to the  people of weldon in the aftermath of the  senseless violence here  welcome back to weldon saskatchewan a  community on edge weldon is just a  pretty tiny prairie village less than  200 people live here everyone knows the  man who was murdered here outside his  home and though there's still plenty of  prairie friendliness here people are  understandably nervous  the idea this tiny village would ever be  in the media spotlight is as foreign to  people here as locking their front doors  the murder of a neighbor in what's  believed to be a random attack has  changed everything i wonder if he  actually ever left the area  tamara shabaga goes to church here and  is doing some painting today  it could have been anybody in town like  everyone here she knew wes peterson i  was supposed to work yesterday here and  i didn't come  obviously because  i was a little nervous about it  um  but i thought you know what life goes on  we have to  not live in fear and  trust the lord that he'll look after us  that it happened here feels unreal  peterson's niece who didn't want to go  on camera said she's still trying to  wrap her head around his murder and the  fact that one of the suspects is still  out there i killed an innocent man for  nothing ruby works says she's nervous  too but wants the world to know her  friend was a good man we're not seeing  anybody out and about in the town  today no  get that terrified  because there's a fear that the other  brother might come back the other  brother might come back and pick another  random house and do it again  we all fear this  and for good reason while tamara shabago  was painting the church another  emergency alert  a suspected sighting of the wanted man  on the james smith first nation that's  just a half hour drive from weldon  that prompted tamara to think twice  about leaving the church open she's  locked the doors and what will make the  community feel more at ease what will  make you feel more at ease  they catch the brother  and put him behind bars  and late today the rcmp said that  suspected sighting on the james smith  cree nation was a false alarm the  suspect has not been found the manhunt  still underway  that is global national for this tuesday  from saskatchewan a province that  remains on high alert thanks for  watching  [Music]  you
 

